preConditionHook:
// THE PERFECT TEST THAT MISSES THE STOPS BY A TICK. The bar's low lands inside
// a marginTicks band around lowest(low, lookback)[1] -- the lowest low of the
// lookback bars ENDING ONE BAR AGO, so the bar is not in its own benchmark --
// and the bar closes up. Mirrored at a high.
//
// EL: priorLow = lowest(low, lookback)[1];
//     low >= priorLow - marginTicks * tickSize and
//     low <= priorLow + marginTicks * tickSize and close > open
//
// A BAND, DELIBERATELY: the point of V16-04 is that price came to the level and
// did NOT trade meaningfully through it, so the lower edge forgives at most
// marginTicks of overshoot and the upper edge requires the bar to have come that
// close at all. Both edges inclusive.
//
// Not SmallBreakoutTrapReversal, which REQUIRES the break (low strictly below
// the level) and the close back above it; not StopRunReversalBar, same
// requirement; not SmallTickFailedBreakoutReversal, the failed poke on two bars.
// The distinction is exactly whether price went through, which is why the merge
// kept them apart.
//
// THE WINDOW IS WRITTEN OUT as the Extremes loop lowest/highest bottom out in --
// seeded at the window's first bar (index 1), scanning the rest with a strict
// comparison; Extremes is EL source, so the comparisons carry the measured
// 2.22e-12 relational tolerance and only the VALUE is used, so the tie rule
// cannot show. The construction BreakoutClearsPriorExtremeByMargin uses.
//
// The tick is written division-first, mirroring EL's MinMove / PriceScale, and
// read once per bar into a local. Every scratch value is a localVariables
// entry, never a hook-scope declaration, so the hooks survive a double
// placement. Deepest bar touched: lookback.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
priorLow = low[1];
priorHigh = high[1];
for (int i = 2; i <= (int)lookback; ++i) {
    if (el_lt(low[i], priorLow)) priorLow = low[i];
    if (el_gt(high[i], priorHigh)) priorHigh = high[i];
}
